# The short game: an agent-played game of Dynasty. Scenario and steering: scenario.md; deck: seed.md.
# Every say and aside is the reply of the agent playing that seat (see log/yearN/).
# The Storyteller writes the act lines and refs the moves; the engine keeps the books.
# The narrator's lines are not part of the game: they live in narration.md, never here.
# Each chapter opens with a "# ===== CHAPTER N: <title>" marker.
setup([1, 2, 3, 4], "short-game", "The short game",
      "Four players · eight rounds · the throne taken in the last round",
      "The Emperor and the three upper provinces, and the table agrees to stop after eight rounds. No peasants and no army: a race for the throne. "
      "Each seller holds the one piece both rivals need, and keeps it back while selling everything else. Clark buys his way onto the "
      "throne in Year 3. Noah finishes a set in Year 5 and takes the Official's chair instead. By the last round Rich and Noah are each "
      "one piece short, and each holds the piece the other needs.",
      short="4 players · 8 rounds")
LAST_ROUND = 8   # the agreed short game (Note 19): briefs.py puts the count on every board card

# ============================== SETUP ==============================
R(0, "Setup", None, "Four players: the Emperor, the Official, the Gentry and the Scholar (rulebook Section 4 and Appendix A). Nobody raids and no soldiers are sold; no peasants, so no rent, and nothing is owed to Provinces 5, 6 or 7. The table agrees to the short game: eight rounds, then score from where everyone sits (Note 19). Each realia province holds one piece of its own realia and two of the one type it never needs.")
act("Four booklets: Reina Osorio draws the Emperor, Clark Grafton the Official, Richard Baron the Gentry and Noah Tall the Scholar.", map_hl=[1,2,3,4])
act("The table agrees to stop after eight rounds and score from where everyone sits.", map_hl=[1,2,3,4])
deal_grain("Rich, the Gentry, sets out the Harvest Pile and deals 60 to each player. It is the first year's harvest.")
take_stock("Clark, Rich and Noah take their realia out of the box: one piece of their own and two of the type they'll never need. It waits beside the board until it's sold.")
act("Clark cuts out the Province 2 Irrigation System, one square by two; Rich the Province 3 Reservoir, two by three. They're set aside until built.", map_hl=[2,3])
act("Noah, the Scholar, shuffles the twenty I Ching cards and lays them face down.", map_hl=[4])
# From here the players' own choices.

# ===== CHAPTER 1: Good Things Come to Those Who Wait
# ============================== YEAR 1 ==============================
R(1, "The first year", None, "No card is drawn. The 60 each player was dealt is the first harvest, and the tax is levied on it.")
act("Before the first tax, the table confirms the short game: play stops after Year 8, and everyone scores from the seat they hold then (Section 18).", map_hl=[1,2,3,4])
CUR_INC.update({s:60 for s in ACTIVE})
say("Reina", "Gentlemen, a light hand to start. Thirty percent, and the crown builds its own Summer Palace out of its own purse. Nobody has to pay for my roof. Clark, Noah, five each for now. That's what the books can carry this year, and I remember who carries the books with me.",
 "Reina: The Summer Palace costs me a third of my grain, but it unlocks the Wall and the Canal, which is where the real bargaining happens. A modest rate keeps the peasants quiet, and keeping salaries flat means none of the three gets a head start on my chair.")
taxes(.30, collector="Clark, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say("Clark", "Thirty percent, Your Majesty, and I'll gather every grain of it: eighteen from Rich, eighteen from Noah, and eighteen from my own purse, all of it in the ledger. Fifty-four to the crown, counted twice. And I'll note that the salary was 'five for now.' I keep a very good record of 'for now.'",
 "Clark: I'm collecting it straight and paying my own share, so nobody at this table can say the tax man cheats. That way, when the Customs House goes up for sale and Rich and Noah both come asking, I'm the clean one holding the only door. And five a year 'for now' goes in the grievance book, page one.")
build("Summer Palace", 20, {1:20}, key="Summer Palace", at="G26", text="Public works: Reina builds the Summer Palace on G26, at the eastern end of Province 1. It must stand before any Wall link or the Province 1 canal.", hover="Reina pays the 20, into the Harvest Pile. 790 of the works are still owed.")
say("Clark", "First lot from the Official's cellar: one Dragon Jar, fine glaze, minimum six. The Customs House stays in the back room for now. Good things come to those who wait, and to those who are polite to the tax man.",
 "Clark: The Customs House is the only one in the game and both of them need it, so I'm not selling it in Year 1. I'll let them stew over it while they spend on something else. A jar up front gets me a little grain and gives me a look at who's bidding hungry.")
say("Rich", "Thirty percent, Your Majesty, a truly gracious hand, and I paid my eighteen with a smile. To honour the occasion I offer the table a Wooden Bowl. Plain, honest, the sort of thing every collection starts with. Eight to open, and a bargain at that.",
 "Rich: The Bowl is the dull piece, which is why it goes first. The Scroll is the one Clark and Noah both need, so it stays in my stock until they've each worked out what it's worth to them, and then they can outbid each other for it.")
say("Noah", "One Vase up this year, minimum eight. Here's where we all stand: eight years, and every one of us still needs four pieces plus twenty grain. Three of us sell three pieces each, so that's nine lots in all, three a year. Nobody is close yet, and I'd rather we all remember that before anyone starts talking about 'for now.'",
 "Noah: I've got two Vases, so selling one first costs me the least, and eight is low enough that somebody bids and high enough that I don't give it away. What I'm really watching is who wants it. That tells me whose set has a Vase in it.")
act("The auctions: each seller chooses which piece from their stock goes up and sets its minimum. Clark puts up a Dragon Jar at 6, Rich a Wooden Bowl at 8, Noah a Vase at 8. Clark, Rich and Noah each give a limit on every lot at once, and the lots are sold in order; the Emperor doesn't bid.", map_hl=[2,3,4])
say("Clark", "A plain honest bowl and a pretty vase. I'll bid on both, gentlemen, but I won't go to war over them. Every grain we throw at each other here goes to the Harvest Pile, and I'd rather keep mine for the tax.",
 "Clark: I need both of these and so does one rival on each lot, so I'm set at twelve apiece: enough to be a real bidder, not so much that I'm broke before the Customs House starts paying off. If Noah takes the bowl or Rich takes the vase, they've spent grain, and I still have the piece they'll both need.")
say("Rich", "My dear Clark, my dear Noah, such lovely pieces, it would be churlish of me not to show some interest. I'll bid like a gentleman and I won't chase anyone into the poorhouse. There's a second Jar and a second Vase still to come, after all, and I'd rather we all came out of this year with grain in our pockets.",
 "Rich: Each of these comes in pairs, so nobody needs to win the first one at any price. I'll go to twelve on each, and if Noah or Clark wants to pay more, most of it ends up in the Harvest Pile, which I'm the one paying out of.")
say("Noah", "The arithmetic, since somebody has to say it: Clark has two Dragon Jars and only Rich and I need one, and Rich has two Wooden Bowls and only Clark and I need one. There's a piece for each of us. If we bid each other up, three quarters of it goes into the Harvest Pile. I'll pay a fair price and stop there, and Rich, I'd suggest you do the same on the Jar.",
 "Noah: Two of each piece, two buyers for each: nothing here is scarce, so I set a sensible ceiling of ten and let a second copy come round next year if I'm outbid. I need twenty grain left at the end, so I'm not spending my whole purse in Year 1.")
DC = "Take it, then. It goes in the ledger as a favour I let you have."
DR = "Too rich for a humble landowner. Congratulations, and enjoy it."
DN = "Go on, take it. There's another one in the cellar, and I can count."
lot(2, "DJ", [B(4,10), B(4,None,DN), B(3,11)],
 hover=TH(("", "Clark's minimum was 6. Noah stopped at 10."),
          ("Rich", "L12 sits deep inside my province, well back from the Wall and the desert, so any raider would have to cross a lot of my land to reach it. A piece I paid eleven for should be the last thing the barbarians can take.")), at="L12")
say("Rich", "Well bid, Clark, a gentleman's victory. Your Jar sits in the heart of my province now, Clark, and I'll care for it like family. Noah, please make a note that the Gentry has started his collection.")
lot(3, "WB", [B(4,10), B(4,None,DN), B(2,11)],
 hover="Rich's minimum was 8. Noah stopped at 10. Clark puts the Bowl on J14.", at="J14")
act("Noah's Vase: Clark and Rich both set 12, and a tie goes to the earlier seat. Each gets one last chance to raise.", map_hl=[2,3])
say("Clark", "Twelve, and I stand there. Rich, you said you'd bid like a gentleman, and the tie is mine. If you want to pay the Harvest Pile for the privilege of beating me, the ledger will record your generosity.",
 "Clark: If Rich holds at twelve, the earlier seat gives me the vase. If he goes over, he's paid a premium, and Noah's second vase comes round next year at a minimum of two. Either way I keep my grain for the twenty I need at the end.")
say("Rich", "I did say I wouldn't chase anyone into the poorhouse, and Rich Baron keeps his word to the letter. The Vase is yours, Clark, and congratulations. Noah, I'll be first in line for the second one, so do keep it polished for me.",
 "Rich: I've got the Jar for eleven and kept thirty-odd grain, and there's a second Vase still to sell, so I'd lose more than I'd gain in a war over this one. And every grain Clark spends on it brings him closer to the tax he'll be collecting from his own purse.")
lot(4, "VA", [B(3,11), B(3,None,DR), B(2,12)],
 hover=TH(("", "Noah's minimum was 8. Both stood at 12, and a tie goes to the earlier seat: Clark, in Province 2."),
          ("Clark", "Two of my four pieces in the first year, both set well back from the Wall and well out of the way. Next I need Rich's Land Deed and Noah's Brush, and the Customs House is still in my back room. Either of them wants it, they'll come to me.")), at="J15")
say("Clark", "A bowl and a vase, both on Clark Grafton's mantelpiece. Thank you, Rich, a gentleman to the letter, and it's noted. Noah, the vase looks better here than it ever did in your cellar.")
salaries({2:5, 4:5}, text="Salaries: Reina pays Clark and Noah 5 each, as she declared.")

# ============================== YEAR 2 ==============================
R(2, "The Emperor's Share", "share")
act("Noah turns the first I Ching card: The Emperor's Share. Every province collects an average harvest, and any province with a public work splits that work's extra harvest with the Emperor. No province has one, so nobody owes a share. The population holds at x1, 100 million.", map_hl=[4])
say("Noah", "The Emperor's Share: 'The Emperor would like to remind you who paid for the ditch.' Average harvest in every province, and any province with a Public Works splits its extra harvest fifty-fifty with the Emperor this round. The counter stays at one hundred million, times one, so read the x1 row. Now the arithmetic: seven years left. Clark holds two pieces, Rich holds one, I hold none. And there's exactly one Customs House, one Land Deed, one Dragon Jar and one Wooden Bowl left beside the board, and I need every one of them. So yes, I'm counting.",
 "Noah: There are no public works anywhere but the crown's palace, so the card is really just Reina reminding us she gets paid. What matters is that every piece I still need is down to its last copy, so from here on each auction is for keeps.")
harvest("Rich, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile: an average year, 35 to each province and 15 to the crown.")
say("Reina", "Thirty again, gentlemen. You can see the crown keeps its word. The first stretch of the Grand Canal goes into my own province on my own grain, and the next time Noah reads us \"the Emperor's Share\", there'll be a ditch to share from. Noah, seven this year: a scholar who counts that carefully has earned the extra two. Clark, your ledger can note that five is still \"for now.\"",
 "Reina: The canal pays me ten a year for the rest of the game, which is the only kind of grain that compounds. Clark has two pieces on the board and Noah has none, so the warm word and the extra two go to Noah. I'd rather the man who's counting kept counting Clark.")
act("Each I Ching card is drawn only once, and The Emperor's Share takes half the bonus only from provinces with works of their own, never from the crown's own canal.", map_hl=[1])
taxes(.30, collector="Clark, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say("Clark", "Ten and a half each, thirty-one and a half to the crown, every half-grain counted. Your Majesty, the ledger now shows seven for the Scholar and five for the Official, who does the actual collecting. I don't complain, I only record. Page two.",
 "Clark: I'll collect it clean again, because an honest tax man is the only kind anybody trusts later. But Reina is paying Noah more than me, and I'm keeping count of that. She'll want something from the Official before this is over.")
build("Grand Canal", 40, {1:40}, canal={1:1}, text="Public works: Reina digs the first section of the Grand Canal in Province 1. It adds 10 to her harvest from next year.", hover="Reina pays the 40, into the Harvest Pile. 750 of the works are still owed.")
say("Clark", "The second Dragon Jar, minimum ten. Noah, you went to ten for its twin last year, so I'm only asking what you've already said it's worth. The Customs House stays in the back room for one more year. It's the only one there is, and I'm in no hurry.",
 "Clark: Noah's the only one who still needs a Jar, so I'm pricing it at his own number from last year. That holds the Customs House back so both of them can sit and think about who controls the last door to the set.")
say("Rich", "Noah, you count so beautifully that I couldn't bear to keep you waiting. My last Wooden Bowl goes up this year, eight to open, and I believe you're the only gentleman at the table who still needs one. Clark, your mantelpiece is quite full enough.",
 "Rich: Noah is the only one who needs the second Bowl, so it sells for about the minimum and no more. My Scroll stays in the cellar a while longer. Clark and Noah both need it, and Noah has just told everyone he's counting the pieces left, so it'll only get more valuable.")
say("Noah", "The second Vase, polished as promised, Rich. Minimum ten. That's what Clark paid for the first, less a little. And for the record: Reina is paying out twelve in salary this year, seven to me and five to Clark, and I'm grateful. I'll also note that my seven is more than the Official's five, Clark, so you can stop glaring at me.",
 "Noah: Rich told the whole table he wants this Vase, so I'm setting a fair minimum and letting him pay it. I need every grain I can get, because every piece I still need is the last one left, and the Customs House and Land Deed will cost real money.")
act("The auctions: Clark puts up his second Dragon Jar at 10, Rich his last Wooden Bowl at 8, Noah his second Vase at 10. All three keep their own single piece in stock one more year: the Customs House, the Land Deed Scroll and the Bamboo Brush are each the last piece left in their stock, and must go up next year.", map_hl=[2,3,4])
say("Clark", "I pass on both, gentlemen. My mantelpiece is full, just as Rich says. Noah, I wasn't glaring, I was reading the books. Seven to you, five to me, and I've written down that you pointed it out yourself.",
 "Clark: I could try to buy those pieces just to block them, but I'd go into next year with too little grain, and next year the Land Deed and the Brush go up, the two pieces I actually need. Let Noah and Rich pay the Harvest Pile now; I'm saving my grain for the real fight.")
say("Rich", "Noah, how lovely of you to keep it polished for me, exactly as you promised. The Jar's all yours, I wouldn't dream of bidding against the only man who needs it. I'm sure you'll return the kindness on the Vase.",
 "Rich: I'm the only one who still needs the Vase, so it should sell near the minimum, but I'll go to eighteen in case Reina or Clark tries to block me. Passing on the Jar costs me nothing and gives me a favour to call in with Noah.")
say("Noah", "I'm the only one at this table who still needs a Jar or a Bowl, so I'll pay the minimums and say thank you. Anyone who bids against me only to block me is paying three grain into the Harvest Pile for every one that reaches the seller, and I'll say so out loud. After this year I'd hold two of my four, and the Customs House and Land Deed go up next year. Six years left.",
 "Noah: Ten and eight should be enough, since nobody else needs these. The higher ceilings are only there in case someone tries to block me. The real fight is next year, over the only Customs House and the only Land Deed, so I want to come out of this year with plenty of grain left.")
act("Each lot draws a single bidder, so each sells at its minimum.", map_hl=[2,3,4])
lot(2, "DJ", [B(4,10)], hover="Clark's minimum was 10, and nobody else bid. Noah puts the Jar on L20.", at="L20")
lot(3, "WB", [B(4,8)],
 hover=TH(("", "Rich's minimum was 8, and nobody else bid."),
          ("Noah", "I put them side by side in the middle of my province, well away from the coast, so nobody can get at them easily. Next year Clark's Customs House and Rich's Land Deed both come up, and they both know I need them, so I'd better have some grain saved.")), at="L21")
say("Noah", "Thank you both. Ten and eight, the minimums, and the Harvest Pile didn't get an extra grain out of anybody. So now: I have two of my four, the Jar and the Bowl. I still need the Customs House and the Land Deed, there's exactly one of each, and both go up next year. Six years left, and I've got 56.5 grain.")
act("Noah in fact holds 59 after the auctions, and 66 once his salary is paid.", map_hl=[4])
lot(4, "VA", [B(3,10)],
 hover=TH(("", "Noah's minimum was 10, and nobody else bid."),
          ("Rich", "That's two of my four, so all I need now is Clark's Customs House and Noah's Bamboo Brush. And I'm sitting on the Scroll that both of them need, so next year's bargaining should be a pleasure.")), at="L13")
say("Rich", "Ten grain, and a happier ten I've never spent. Noah, it'll sit right beside the Jar where I can admire them both. You are a gentleman and a scholar, quite literally.")
salaries({2:5, 4:7}, text="Salaries: Reina pays Clark 5 and Noah 7, as she declared.")

# ===== CHAPTER 2: The Most Expensive Receipt in the Empire
# ============================== YEAR 3 ==============================
R(3, "Heaven Has Favourites", "heaven")
act("Year 3 of the eight the table agreed. Play stops after Year 8, and everyone scores from the seat they hold then (Note 19).", map_hl=[1,2,3,4])
act("Noah turns the next I Ching card: Heaven Has Favourites. A good harvest in Provinces 2 and 4, a bad one in Provinces 1 and 3. The population holds at x1, 100 million.", map_hl=[4])
say("Noah", "\"Heaven Has Favourites. Some fields shine, some fields sulk.\" Good harvest in Provinces 2, 4 and 5, so that's Clark and me, and 5 sits empty. Bad harvest in 1, 3, 6, 7 and 8, so that's Reina and Rich. The population stays at x1 and the counter reads 100 million, so everyone reads the x1 row. And the numbers, since that's my job: this is Year 3 of 8, so five rounds remain after this one. Clark, Rich and I each hold two pieces and each need two more. Nobody is one piece away yet. The Customs House, the Land Deed and the Brush are the last copies, one of each, and every one of them is needed by two of us.",
 "Noah: I'll admit it's awkward reading out a card that's kind to me and unkind to Rich in the same year he's selling me the Land Deed. I'd rather say that out loud myself than have someone else point it out.")
harvest("Rich, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile: a good year for Clark and Noah, 60 each; a bad one for Rich, 10; and for the crown a bad harvest less its 60, with the canal's 10, nothing.")
say("Reina", "Thirty again. Heaven has been unkind to my fields and Rich's, and I won't make a bad year worse by squeezing the good ones. The second stretch of canal goes in, out of my own purse as usual. Clark, you'll find two more in your envelope this year. I said five was for now, and I keep my word, a little at a time. Noah, you count beautifully, but this year the man who carried the tax gets the extra.",
 "Reina: With that canal finished, my own fields earn twenty more a year until the end, and all of it comes from building, none from taxing them. I gave the extra two to Clark because Noah is sitting richest and closest to my chair this year, and a gentle tax keeps poor Rich too busy surviving to think about revolt.")
taxes(.30, collector="Clark, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say("Clark", "Thirty percent, thirty-nine in all: eighteen from me, paid first as always, three from Rich, and eighteen from you, Noah. Count it out on the table where we can all see it. And Your Majesty, seven, how generous. I'll put it in the ledger, just below \"five for now\", so the page reads nicely from top to bottom.",
 "Clark: Seven's a start, and I'm crossing out nothing. She paid me ahead of Noah in front of everyone, so the crown needs its tax man more than it needs its counter. Meanwhile I'm the only one with the Customs House, and Rich and Noah are each sitting on a piece I need. That's three people who'll want to be friendly this year.")
build("Grand Canal", 40, {1:40}, canal={1:1}, text="Public works: Reina digs the second section of the Grand Canal in Province 1, finishing it there. It adds 10 more to her harvest from next year, 20 in all.", hover="Reina pays the 40, into the Harvest Pile. 710 of the works are still owed.")
say("Clark", "Gentlemen, the one and only Customs House goes up at last, opening at forty. It's the last one in the empire, and you both need it, so I wouldn't think of letting it go cheap. Noah, you have the deeper purse. Rich, you have the bigger grudge. Let's see which counts for more.",
 "Clark: If Noah pays forty, he's that much poorer and I can buy what I need. If nobody bids, I keep the only piece both of them need for another year. Either way the minimum works for me.")
say("Rich", "My dear Clark, how right you are that the last of anything deserves a proper price. In that spirit, my humble Land Deed goes up at forty, the very same as your fine Customs House, so nobody can say Rich asked a penny more than the Official. Noah, Clark, you both need it and there is only the one. I'm sure two such gentlemen will be generous with each other.",
 "Rich: That's my only piece, so it goes up whether I like it or not. Forty matches Clark's own price, so he can't call it greed. Whichever of them wins the Scroll buys me the grain to chase their Customs House or their Brush, and I'll remember which one of them was nice to me about it.")
say("Noah", "Then the Bamboo Brush goes up at forty too, so all three last pieces open at the same price and nobody has to wonder whose is dearer. Here's the arithmetic: I hold 108 and have to keep 20, which leaves me 88. Both of your lots at forty comes to eighty. Clark, Rich, each of you needs my Brush and one of the other's pieces, so you'll each want at least eighty as well. Whoever is short of that won't finish this year.",
 "Noah: Forty for forty for forty is the only fair number, and it also means I'm paid on the way in for what I'll spend on the way out. If either of them balks at my price, they're balking at their own.")
act("The auctions: each seller has a single piece left, so each goes up. Clark's Customs House, Rich's Land Deed Scroll and Noah's Bamboo Brush each open at 40. A seller keeps a quarter of the price. Clark, Rich and Noah each give a limit on every lot at once; the Emperor doesn't bid.", map_hl=[2,3,4])
say("Clark", "Forty, forty, forty, all very neat, Noah, you've done the arithmetic for all of us. Here's mine: I paid for every grain I hold honestly, and I mean to finish the set while the rest of you are still checking your sums. Rich, Noah, either of you is welcome to try to outbid the tax man.",
 "Clark: If Noah pays forty for my Customs House, he's financing my run at his own Brush, which is exactly what I'd hoped. I'll go a little over the minimum on both pieces I need, and a few grains more only to stop the man who'd be one piece short behind me.")
say("Rich", "Noah, your sums are as elegant as ever, and you're quite right, poor Rich can't stretch to eighty in a lean year. So I'll do the modest thing and make Clark's Customs House the one purchase of my season, at his own fine price. As for the Brush, I bow out, and I wish its buyer joy of it.",
 "Rich: I can't afford a whole set this year, but if I hold the only Customs House, Noah can't finish either. That leaves my Scroll and Noah's Brush as Clark's road, and Clark will owe a man like me some courtesy for that.")
say("Noah", "Here's my side of it in numbers. I have 108 and owe the end of the game 20, so I've got 88 to bid with, and I've split it between the two lots I need. Rich, you hold 57, so you can't take both the Customs House and my Brush at forty each this year. Clark, you hold 100 and a half, so you can take the Deed and the Brush at forty each and still keep your twenty. That means you're the one to watch, and I'd be lying if I said otherwise.",
 "Noah: Forty-six and forty-two adds up to exactly my eighty-eight, so if I win both I still keep my twenty and finish this year. Rich can only outbid me on the Customs House by giving up my Brush, and Clark can only beat me on the Deed by paying over forty-two for it.")
DR3 = "Too rich for a humble Gentry. Congratulations, sir, it's in worthy hands."
DN3 = "That's past my number. I said my limit out loud and I'll stick to it. Congratulations."
lot(2, "CH", [B(3,40), B(3,None,DR3), B(4,41)],
 hover=TH(("", "Clark's minimum was 40. Rich stopped at 40; Noah would have gone to 46."),
          ("Noah", "That's three times I've lost a lot by a single grain. Each time I'd been honest about my number, and each time someone used it. Clark's complete now, so from here on the real question is what Rich and I can do together to stop him.")), at="L22")
lot(3, "LD", [B(4,42), B(4,None,DN3), B(2,43)],
 hover="Rich's minimum was 40. Noah stopped at 42; Clark would have gone to 48 to keep it from him. Clark puts the Deed on J13.", at="J13")
lot(4, "BB", [B(2,40)],
 hover=TH(("", "Noah's minimum was 40, and nobody else bid."),
          ("Clark", "My set is finished, so the four pieces sit in a neat row on J13 to J16. Everything I wrote down for three years is about to come due.")), at="J16")
say("Clark", "The Deed goes here, the Brush goes there, and the Bowl and the Vase get some company. Four of four, gentlemen, with twenty-seven and a half in the purse, which is more than twenty. Noah, enjoy the Customs House. It's the most expensive receipt in the empire.")
say("Noah", "The Customs House goes on L22, beside the Jar and the Bowl. The arithmetic needs saying, so I'll say it. I lost the Deed by one grain, again, and Clark now holds all four of his pieces with 27 and a half grain, which clears the twenty he has to keep. Clark is finished. I'm one piece short, and the last Land Deed belongs to him. Rich still needs the Customs House and the Brush, and those have gone too. There are five rounds left.")
say("Clark", "Your Majesty, I've been keeping a little book. Page one says \"five for now\", and page two says \"seven, how generous\". I'm declaring the set: Bowl, Vase, Deed and Brush. I'll take the throne, thank you. Reina, the Official's seat is very comfortable, and I'll pay you at least five a year for it, and maybe seven if you're polite to the new tax man.")
swap(2, 1, "Clark Grafton declares a complete set (Wooden Bowl, Vase, Land Deed Scroll, Bamboo Brush) and 27.5 grain, and takes the throne. Clark becomes Emperor of Province 1; Reina Osorio becomes Official of Province 2. A new Emperor returns all his realia: the Land Deed Scroll and the Wooden Bowl go back to Province 3's stock, the Bamboo Brush and the Vase to Province 4's, to be auctioned again. Clark takes the crown's reserve of 77.5; Reina draws 60 from the Harvest Pile.",
 hover="Clark: I'm in Province 2 and the throne is Province 1, so this one move wins my game as long as I hold the crown for five more years. Nobody else can finish a set now, because I bought the last Deed and the last Brush. The ledger's closed, and it balanced in my favour.")
say("Clark", "The Official gets seven, Reina. It turns out that's the going rate for carrying the tax, so I'm told. Noah, five for now, for reading the card aloud. You'll find the crown keeps its word, a little at a time.",
 "Clark: The Deed is back in Rich's stock and Noah is one piece short, so every grain I leave in Noah's purse is a grain he can bid against my throne. He gets the minimum, and Reina gets my old seven, which I'm sure she'll appreciate.")
salaries({2:7, 4:5}, text="Salaries: Clark, now Emperor, pays Reina, now Official, 7 and Noah 5.")

# ============================== YEAR 4 ==============================
R(4, "The Wall Is Breached", "breach")
act("Noah turns the next I Ching card: The Wall Is Breached. A good harvest in every province. No Wall link has been built, so Provinces 1, 2 and 3 each pay 2 in damages instead of 5 for repairs. Province 4 plays a personal disaster. The population holds at x1, 100 million.", map_hl=[4])
say("Noah", "\"The Wall Is Breached.\" Good harvest everywhere this year. Three links of the Wall are down, so Provinces 1, 2 and 3 each pay 5 for repairs, or 2 to the Harvest Pile if there's no wall built above them. That's Clark, Reina and Rich. Provinces 4 through 8 play a Personal Disaster, and of us that's only me, so I'll turn the next page of my booklet. The population stays at x1 and the counter reads 100 million. Now the arithmetic for Year 4 of 8, with four rounds left after this one. Clark holds the throne. I hold three pieces and 82 grain, and the one thing I'm missing is a Land Deed, which is now back in Rich's stock. Rich, you and I should talk in public before the auctions, not after.",
 "Noah: Clark won the throne and handed the Deed straight back to Rich, so my problem has stopped being about the price. Now it's about whether Rich would rather see me on the throne or Clark.")
harvest("Rich, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile: a good year for all. 60 to each province, and 60 to the crown with its two canal sections.")
act("The card's levy: no Wall stands above Provinces 1, 2 or 3, so Clark, Reina and Rich each pay 2 to the Harvest Pile for damages.", deltas={1:-2,2:-2,3:-2}, map_hl=[1,2,3], pile_delta=6)
disaster(4, "a leak in the library roof soaks a winter's copying, and the Scholar pays to have it done again.")
say("Clark", "A bountiful year all round, so the crown asks for half. Fat harvests pay fat taxes, I'm sure you'll agree. The Winter Palace goes up out of the crown's purse, so I'll have somewhere warm to keep the ledgers. Reina, seven again for collecting it. Noah, five, and you can count it yourself.",
 "Clark: Noah's only one Land Deed from my throne, and every thirty grain I tax out of him is thirty he can't bid at Rich's auction. And the Winter Palace is the key that unlocks his canal, so he'll be wanting to talk nicely to me soon. Whispered to Rich: \"Rich, the Deed going to Noah is the one thing that unseats me. Set your minimum on it high this year, or let it sit in stock, and I'll make the Reservoir in Province 3 the crown's next project with the crown paying the larger share, and you'll have a friend on the throne for four years.\"")
taxes(.50, collector="Reina, now the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say("Reina", "Fifty percent it is: thirty from me, paid first, the way the Official does it, thirty from Rich and thirty from Noah. Ninety for the crown, counted on the table. Clark, you held that ledger for two years, so you'll understand if I start one of my own. Page one: 'half, in the first year.'",
 "Reina: I put him on that chair to begin with, and he's already taxing at a rate I never touched once. A crown that takes half in a good year makes enemies quickly, and I'll let him make them. Rich holds the Bowl and the Deed, and Noah holds the Vase and the Brush, so I'll be very warm to both of them until the auctions open.")
build("Winter Palace", 20, {1:20}, key="Winter Palace", at="H20", text="Public works: Clark builds the Winter Palace on H20, in Province 1. It must stand before the canals in Provinces 4, 5 and 6.", hover="Clark pays the 20, into the Harvest Pile. 690 of the works are still owed.")
say("Rich", "Long live Emperor Clark! Your Majesty, a fine harvest and a finer throne. Noah, I heard you, and I'm happy to talk in public: this year the humble Gentry offers his Wooden Bowl, at thirty, a fair price for a piece with such distinguished history. The Deed stays home this year to rest. It's had a very tiring career.",
 "Rich: Nobody needs the Bowl, so at thirty it'll sit unsold and go up again next year at two. The Deed doesn't have to go up until Year 6, and that holds Noah back while the new Emperor pays for my Reservoir. Whispered to Clark: \"Your Majesty, the Deed does not go up this year, my word on it, exactly as worded. I'll expect the Reservoir in Province 3 to be the crown's next project, with the crown paying the larger share. Keep your end and we'll talk again before the Deed has to see daylight.\"")
say("Noah", "Then the Vase goes up, at ten, and the Brush rests at home beside your Deed, Rich. Here's the arithmetic for everyone: a piece can be held back for one year but never two, so the Deed has to come up next year, and so does my Brush. I'll say it now so nobody's surprised. The Deed is the only thing between me and a full set, and the Brush is something you need. I'd be glad to settle the pair of them in public, a fair trade at a fair price, before we hand the Harvest Pile another bidding war.",
 "Noah: Rich has shown me how the game works now, so I've done the same back to him. Both of our pieces come up next year whether we like it or not, so either we make a deal or we make a fight.")
act("The one-year limit applies only to a lot that drew no bid; putting up the other piece isn't holding one back. If Rich's Bowl sells this year, the Deed is his last piece and goes up next year; if the Bowl goes unsold, the Bowl reopens next year at 2 and the Deed can wait a year more. The same holds for Noah's Vase and Brush.", map_hl=[3,4])
act("The auctions: Province 2's stock is empty, so two lots. Rich puts up the Wooden Bowl at 30, Noah the Vase at 10. Reina, Rich and Noah each give a limit on every lot at once; the Emperor doesn't bid.", map_hl=[3,4])
say("Reina", "Rich, Noah, you'll have noticed I'm the only person at this table who still needs a Bowl or a Vase, so there's no war to be had here, just a fair price at your own minimums. I'm starting from nothing, gentlemen, and an Official with an empty shelf hasn't got much grudge left to spend. And Noah, when the Deed and the Brush come up next year, I'd be glad to talk in public, exactly as you suggest.",
 "Reina: Nobody else needs these two pieces, so I'll take them cheap now while the others are still circling the Deed, and have half a set before Clark has finished counting his half-tax. He took my chair with four pieces and twenty-seven and a half grain, and I'm going to take it back the same way.")
say("Rich", "Noah, what a generous offer, and so well counted. But I'm afraid a Brush is a sad thing to own without a Customs House to keep it in, and the only Customs House in the empire is sitting on your L22. When we talk Deeds, let's talk about the whole of what each of us gets, in public, next year. The Bowl stays at thirty, and Lady Reina, I'd be honoured to see it in your collection.",
 "Rich: Noah's trade gets me a Brush I can't use and gets him the throne, so no thank you, not at that price. If Reina pays thirty I get seven and a half, and if she doesn't, the Bowl comes back next year and the Deed waits another year, as I promised Clark.")
say("Noah", "I stand corrected, and thank you, Storyteller. I had the hold-back rule wrong, so that's twice now I've misstated something in public, and I'd rather own it than have it pointed out. Here's the right version: if Rich's Bowl sells this year, the Deed is his last piece and has to come up next year. So I'm passing on the Bowl, and Reina, it's yours to win if you want it. The offer to Rich stands, in public: the Brush and the Deed, settled fairly, whenever he's ready.",
 "Noah: I'm hoping somebody buys that Bowl. Every grain Reina spends on it pushes Rich's Deed onto next year's table, and I'll have 108 waiting when it gets there.")
act("Each lot draws a single bidder, Reina, so each sells at its minimum.", map_hl=[2,3,4])
lot(3, "WB", [B(2,30)], hover="Rich's minimum was 30, and nobody else bid. Reina puts the Bowl on J14.", at="J14")
lot(4, "VA", [B(2,10)],
 hover=TH(("", "Noah's minimum was 10, and nobody else bid."),
          ("Reina", "Two pieces down and two to go, both of them up next year. I put them on Clark's old squares so that every time he looks at Province 2 he remembers how he got his chair.")), at="J15")
say("Reina", "The Bowl goes on J14 and the Vase on J15, exactly where Clark kept his own, so they ought to feel at home. Thank you, Rich, and thank you, Noah. You both priced them like gentlemen.")
act("With the Bowl and the Vase sold, the Land Deed Scroll is the last piece in Rich's stock and the Bamboo Brush the last in Noah's: both go up next year.", map_hl=[3,4])
salaries({2:7, 4:5}, text="Salaries: Clark pays Reina 7 and Noah 5, as he declared.")

# ===== CHAPTER 3: Ninety-Five Fits
# ============================== YEAR 5 ==============================
R(5, "Utility Tax", "utility")
act("Year 5 of the eight the table agreed. Play stops after Year 8, and everyone scores from the seat they hold then (Note 19).", map_hl=[1,2,3,4])
act("Noah turns the next I Ching card: Utility Tax. An average harvest in every province, and every province pays the Emperor 2, and 2 more if it has a public work of its own. Only the crown's own province has works, so Reina, Rich and Noah each pay Clark 2. The population holds at x1, 100 million.", map_hl=[4])
say("Noah", "Utility Tax. Every province brings in an average harvest. Then every province pays the Emperor 2, and any province with a Public Works pays 2 more. Population stays at x1, so the counter reads 100 million. Everyone reads the x1 row. Now the count, as usual. This is Year 5 of 8, so after this year there are three left. I need the Land Deed and 20 grain, nothing else. Reina needs the Deed and my Brush. Both of those go up this year, so Rich and I are the ones selling what decides the game. Clark, that's 2 a province to you, and 5 to me at year's end.",
 "Noah: A quiet card, and I'm glad of it. No disaster gets in the way, so this year is all about the Deed, and I'll say my number before anyone else does.")
harvest("Rich, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile: an average year, 35 to each province, and 35 to the crown with its two canal sections.")
act("The Utility Tax: Reina, Rich and Noah each pay Clark 2. The crown's own works are in its own province, so nobody pays the extra 2.", deltas={2:6,1:-2,3:-2,4:-2}, map_hl=[1,2,3,4])
say("Clark", "Sixty percent, ladies and gentlemen. Somebody has to pay for all this lovely infrastructure. Rich, the crown keeps its word: the Reservoir goes in this year, and the crown pays fifty-five of the eighty. Twenty-five from you buys twenty more grain every harvest for the rest of the game. Noah, your two from the Utility Tax is received and entered, and you get five, same as ever. Reina, seven. I don't forget a precedent.",
 "Clark: Every grain I tax out of Noah is a grain he can't bid on that Deed, and every grain of Reservoir I pay for reminds Rich who his friends are. It's the same lever from both ends, and I've got both hands on it. Whispered to Rich: \"The Reservoir's paid, as promised. Your harvest goes up by twenty a year. Put the Deed up if you have to, but set the minimum high, and if Noah's the only one bidding, the crown will bid against him and make it worth your while. Keep me in the chair and there'll be a Great Wall link over Province 3 on the same terms.\"")
taxes(.60, collector="Reina, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say("Reina", "Twenty-one from me, twenty-one from Rich, twenty-one from Noah: sixty-three to the crown, counted twice and short by nothing. Clark, I collected fifty for you last year and sixty this year without anyone throwing a cup at me, but I've sat in that chair and I know what comes after sixty. Spend the Reservoir money well, because the table is keeping its own ledger now.",
 "Reina: Every point he puts on the tax is a point of goodwill he's spending for me. I'll hand him every grain correctly, so when the table turns on him nobody can say the Official was the one who cheated them.")
say("Rich", "Your Majesty, twenty-five for a Reservoir is a gift, and I accept it with both hands and a full heart. Province 3 will drink to the crown every harvest. As for the Scroll, it's the last one in the empire, gentlemen and lady, so it goes up at ninety-five. I'd be insulting its history to ask a penny less.",
 "Rich: Twenty-five grain buys me sixty back over the last three harvests, and that's the best trade I'll make all game. Ninety-five means that if Noah wants his set this year, he pays my price and is left counting his last few grains in public.")
build("Province 3 Reservoir", 80, {1:55, 3:25}, key="res3", at="K16:L18", text="Public works: Clark and Rich build the Province 3 Reservoir on K16:L18, the crown paying 55 and Rich 25. It adds 20 to Rich's harvest from next year.", hover="The 80 goes into the Harvest Pile. 610 of the works are still owed.")
say("Noah", "The Brush goes up at forty, the same price Clark paid for the last one. Same price for the same piece, so nobody can say I priced it to suit myself. And Rich, ninety-five is steep, but I said I'd pay a steep price and tell you what it cost me. I have 127 and a half, I keep 20 back, so 107 and a half is the most I can spend. Ninety-five fits.",
 "Noah: Reina is the only one who really needs that Brush, and forty will cost her if she wants it, which keeps her away from the Deed. As for Rich's ninety-five, I've lost three lots by one grain each, and I'd rather overpay than make it four.")
act("The auctions: Province 2's stock is empty, so two lots. Rich puts up the Land Deed Scroll at 95, Noah the Bamboo Brush at 40. Reina, Rich and Noah each give a limit on every lot at once; the Emperor doesn't bid.", map_hl=[3,4])
say("Reina", "Ninety-five for a scroll, Rich, and I have seventy-four. I can count, so I'll let Noah have the history lesson. The Brush is a different matter. Forty is a fair price, Noah, and I'll pay it. Somebody at this table ought to own a pen they didn't steal.",
 "Reina: I can't reach the Deed this year, so I'm making sure the Brush doesn't go to Rich and finish him off. A piece in my hand is something to bargain with, and I'd rather have two men needing something from me than one man on my old throne thanking me for staying out of it.")
say("Rich", "Ninety-five it stays, Noah. You named the figure fits, and I'd never be the man who made a scholar's sums come out wrong. Reina, the Brush is all yours with my blessing. A pen in the Official's hand is a comfort to us all.",
 "Rich: The Customs House is glued to Noah's board, so a Brush would only be a handsome paperweight for me, and once the Deed goes to Noah, Reina's Brush can't finish anything either. I keep my grain and let the two of them pay the Harvest Pile I hand out.")
say("Noah", "Ninety-five for the Deed, Rich, and I'm taking it. My ceiling is 107, which leaves me 20 and a half, just over the 20 I have to keep. Reina, the Brush is yours at forty, as I said. Three quarters of that goes to the Pile and a quarter comes back to me, so it's a fair price both ways.",
 "Noah: Reina has 74, so she can't reach the 95 minimum, and the Emperor doesn't bid. That means the Deed comes to me at the minimum, and I'll hold all four pieces with 32 and a half grain left over.")
act("Each lot draws a single bidder at its minimum: Noah for the Land Deed Scroll, Reina for the Bamboo Brush.", map_hl=[2,3,4])
lot(3, "LD", [B(4,95)],
 hover=TH(("", "Rich's minimum was 95, and nobody else bid."),
          ("Noah", "Four years of losing by one grain, and in the end it went at the minimum with nobody bidding. The set is complete and the arithmetic works, so all I have to do now is pick my moment.")), at="L23")
say("Noah", "The Deed goes on L23, next to the Customs House. That makes four pieces: Jar, Bowl, Customs House, Deed. I have 42 and a half grain against the 20 I have to keep. So I hold a full set, and three years are left. Clark, you've got 258 grain and a throne, and the table can see I can now come for both.")
lot(4, "BB", [B(2,40)],
 hover=TH(("", "Noah's minimum was 40, and nobody else bid."),
          ("Reina", "Noah has his set, and the only Deed left in the empire is sitting in his province, so my road back to the throne goes through him and not around him. If he takes Clark's chair, I want to be the first face he sees when he needs an Official he can trust.")), at="J16")
say("Reina", "J16, in a row with the Bowl and the Vase, so it's tidy. Noah, congratulations on the Deed. You have four pieces and more than twenty grain, so the whole table knows what you can do now. When you take a chair, remember who stood aside at ninety-five and didn't make you pay a hundred and seven.")
say("Noah", "I'll say the arithmetic out loud, since that's my job. If I take the throne, the Deed goes back to Rich's stock and has to come up again. Reina needs only that one piece, so she'd take the throne from me by Year 7. If I take Province 2, I keep the Deed, and nobody at this table is one piece away any more. Reina, I'm sorry. You didn't stand aside at ninety-five, you told us yourself you had seventy-four. I'm taking the Official's chair, and Province 2 beats the 4 I drew.")
swap(4, 2, "Noah Tall declares a complete set (Dragon Jar, Wooden Bowl, Customs House, Land Deed Scroll) and 42.5 grain, and replaces Reina Osorio as the Official. Noah becomes Official of Province 2; Reina becomes Scholar of Province 4. The Customs House, native to Province 2, goes back to its stock; Noah's other three pieces move with him to J14, J15 and J16. Reina returns everything she held: the Wooden Bowl to Province 3's stock, the Vase and the Bamboo Brush to Province 4's. Noah keeps his grain and Reina's 34; Reina draws 60 from the Harvest Pile.",
 hover="Noah: The throne is the bigger prize, but it would hand Reina a clear road to take it off me. Taking her seat ends the only threat on the board, and three years is a long time to keep a seat nobody else can reach.",
 at={"DJ":"J14", "WB":"J15", "LD":"J16"})
say("Clark", "Well! The seats have moved, and the salary goes with the seat, or at least most of it does. Noah, you get five as the Official. That's what you've had from me every year, and I'd hate to confuse you with a change. Reina, seven to the Scholar. It's what I promised the Official this year, and you're the one who earned it. I've written a new page in the book, and Noah's name is at the top of it.",
 "Clark: Noah went past the throne to take Reina's chair, and that tells me he's afraid of her and doesn't want my seat yet. So Reina gets the two extra grain and my sympathy, and I get someone at the table with a fresh grudge against him.")
salaries({2:5, 4:7}, text="Salaries: Clark pays Noah, now the Official, 5 and Reina, now the Scholar, 7.")

# ============================== YEAR 6 ==============================
R(6, "Population Doubles", "double3")
act("Reina, now the Scholar, turns the next I Ching card: Population Doubles. A good harvest in every province, and the population doubles to x2, 200 million: every province now eats twice as much. From this year, new land may be bought.", map_hl=[4])
say("Reina", "Population Doubles. 'Fat years make large families.' Every province gets a good harvest, and then the population doubles and so does everyone's consumption. The counter goes from one hundred million to two hundred million, so we're at times two now. Everyone, read the x2 row, and don't count the good harvest until you've doubled what you eat. Noah, enjoy my chair. I left it warm and the books balanced, which is more than I was given. Clark, Province 4 is now hungrier than it is rich, and a crown that wants a Scholar alive next year might think about paying for a square or two of new land.",
 "Reina: I've been thrown out of two chairs in three years, and I'm sitting in the province furthest from the throne with no pieces at all. The game is about grain now, so I'll make the crown pay for my land and keep my purse whole for the tiebreak.")
harvest("Rich, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile: a good year, but everyone now eats twice as much. 20 to Noah and Reina, 40 to Rich with the new Reservoir, and nothing to the crown, whose canal bonus just covers its doubled consumption.")
say("Clark", "Fat years make large families, and a large family wants feeding, so the crown is opening the granary. Tax comes down to forty, and the Pagoda goes up so the empire has something to look at while it eats. On new land the crown pays half of every square for Province 2 and Province 3. Reina, the crown pays three quarters of yours, since the Scholar wants keeping alive, as she very reasonably pointed out. Noah, it's five again. The Official's chair came with a tradition and I've kept it.",
 "Clark: Revolts start in hungry bellies, so I'm buying grain for the people who might vote me out, and Reina gets the biggest share so she stays grateful and stays angry at Noah. Four squares for the crown puts my own income back in the black before the next doubling comes. Whispered to Reina: \"Noah took your chair and your pieces. The crown has a long memory and a large reserve. If he goes for the throne, tell me early, and Province 4 won't go hungry while I'm sitting here.\"")
act("Provinces 2 and 3 can't buy new land yet: their only new land is the strip below the Wall, which opens only when all four links above each of them stand, and none is built. This year only Province 1 (the sea) and Province 4 (the sea) can buy.", map_hl=[1,2,3,4])
taxes(.40, collector="Noah, now the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say("Noah", "Forty percent, as declared. Rich pays 16 on his 40, Reina 8 on her 20, and I pay 8 on my 20. That's 32 to the crown, and I've counted it twice. For the record, this is Year 6 of 8, so three years are left including this one. Reina has started again from nothing, Rich is the closest of anyone to a set and still isn't close, and Province 2 loses about 5 grain in an average year, so I'll be watching my own ledger as closely as everyone else's.",
 "Noah: The Official's chair turns out to be hungry. Province 2 can't buy land until the Wall above it is built, so the 101 and a half I have is what I've got to last me. As long as nobody puts a set together in two years, Province 2 is mine at the end.")
build("Marble Pagoda", 20, {1:20}, key="Marble Pagoda", at="H22", text="Public works: Clark builds the Marble Pagoda on H22, in Province 1. It's the only work this year.", hover="Clark pays the 20, into the Harvest Pile. 590 of the works are still owed.")
buy_land(1, ["F23", "F24", "G27", "H25"], {1:80}, "New land: Clark buys four squares of sea for Province 1, along the coast at F23, F24, G27 and H25, from the crown's own reserve. Each adds 20 to the crown's harvest from next year.", hover="The 80 goes into the Harvest Pile.")
say("Reina", "Two squares of sea, J25 and K25. That's forty, and on the crown's three quarters I pay ten and Clark pays thirty. Thank you, Your Majesty. That's the first roof anyone has helped pay for in this game, and I'll make sure the table hears about it.",
 "Reina: Two squares cost me ten grain and bring me forty a year, so Province 4 stops starving and my surplus grows while the others pay for their ambitions. Clark wants an informant, and he can have one, because Noah on the throne would be worse for me than Clark staying on it. Whispered to Clark: \"Agreed. If Noah or anyone else starts building a set aimed at you, you'll hear it from me before it reaches the auction table. In exchange, Province 4 is fed and the Scholar's salary stays at seven.\"")
buy_land(4, ["J25", "K25"], {4:10, 1:30}, "New land: Reina buys two squares of sea for Province 4, at J25 and K25, the crown paying 30 of the 40. Each adds 20 to her harvest from next year.", hover="The 40 goes into the Harvest Pile.")
say("Noah", "The Customs House goes up at 150, and I'll tell you why, because I'm not going to pretend otherwise. Rich, you have the Jar and the Vase, and you once told me a Brush means nothing to you without the Customs House. That makes this the piece that puts you within reach of my chair. So I'm pricing it where nobody will pay, and the rules say it comes up at 2 next year. That's the one year's hold the rules allow me, and I'm using it in public.",
 "Noah: Holding it back only buys me a year, but at the end of Year 8 a year is a third of what's left. If Rich wants my seat, he'll need the Brush and the Customs House both by Year 8, and I can bid against him on the Brush.")
say("Rich", "Noah, you've taught us all how it's done, and I'm an eager pupil. The Bowl goes up at one hundred and fifty, the same figure as your Customs House, so nobody can say Province 3 priced a piece to suit itself. I kept my word to the letter all game, and I'll take the one year's hold the rules allow me, in public, the same as you.",
 "Rich: Reina has to rebuild a whole set from nothing, and the Bowl is a piece she can't get anywhere else, so it sits on my shelf this year and comes up at 2 next year, when the clock is nearly out. If Noah freezes his House, I freeze my Bowl, and everyone stays where they are, which suits Province 3 down to the ground.")
say("Reina", "I'm putting the Vase up at ten, which is an honest price for something nobody at this table needs, and I'm keeping the Brush in its box for now. I'll also take two more squares of sea, I25 and J26, on the same terms as before if the crown is willing. Ten from me, thirty from Clark, and the Scholar's province eats through whatever the next card brings.",
 "Reina: Two players have now held pieces back with a hundred and fifty minimum, and I'm doing the same with the Brush, only quietly: it's the piece Rich needs most, so it goes up last. Meanwhile every square of sea the crown pays for is grain in my purse when the table counts surpluses in Year 8.")
act("Noah, now the Official, needs the Vase: his set is the Wooden Bowl, the Vase, the Land Deed and the Bamboo Brush, and he holds the Bowl and the Deed.", map_hl=[2])
buy_land(4, ["I25", "J26"], {4:10, 1:30}, "New land: Reina buys two more squares of sea for Province 4, at I25 and J26, the crown paying 30 of the 40 on the terms Clark declared.", hover="The 40 goes into the Harvest Pile.")
act("The auctions: three lots. Noah puts up the Customs House at 150, Rich the Wooden Bowl at 150, Reina the Vase at 10. Noah, Rich and Reina each give a limit on every lot at once; the Emperor doesn't bid.", map_hl=[2,3,4])
say("Noah", "The Customs House stays at 150, as I announced. I'm passing on the Bowl, since I already have one. The Vase I'll take, and my ceiling is 20. That's double Reina's minimum and as much as I'll pay for a piece I can live without. Here's the count: if I win it at 10, I'm left with 83 and a half, and Province 2 loses about 5 an average year, so I'm fine to the end of Year 8.",
 "Noah: With the Vase I'd hold three of the four pieces for a second set, and only Reina's Brush missing. That isn't to win Province 2, I already hold it. It's a way to take the throne in Year 8 if Clark gets careless.")
say("Rich", "One hundred and fifty for the Customs House, Noah, and I have a hundred and thirty-seven and a half, so I can count too and I'll bow out with my compliments. The Bowl stays at one-fifty, as promised. As for Reina's lovely Vase, I'm a collector at heart, and a man can't have too many.",
 "Rich: A second Vase is no use to me, but on Noah's shelf it puts him a Brush away from the throne, so I'll pay a little over his twenty to keep it from him. Clark sees who kept him in the chair, and three quarters of my grain goes into the Pile I pay out of anyway.")
say("Reina", "Pass, and pass. A hundred and fifty twice over is more than my whole purse, and I'd rather keep my grain than buy pieces I can't use. The Vase stays at ten, as I said. Bid as you like, gentlemen. And note that the Brush is still in my stock, and both of you want it.",
 "Reina: The Brush is the one piece both Noah and Rich need, and I'm the only person at the table who can sell it. Whoever gets the Vase, the man who wants the throne in Year 8 will have to come and talk to me first.")
act("Nobody meets Noah's 150 for the Customs House or Rich's 150 for the Wooden Bowl. Both stay in stock, and both must go up again next year with the minimum at 2.", map_hl=[2,3])
DN6 = "Past my number. I said my limit out loud, and I'm sticking to it."
lot(4, "VA", [B(2,20), B(2,None,DN6), B(3,21)],
 hover=TH(("", "Reina's minimum was 10. Noah stopped at 20; Rich would have gone to 25 to keep it from him."),
          ("Rich", "It goes on L14, next to its twin, where it does nothing except stay off Noah's shelf, and that was the reason I bought it. Next year the Bowl and the Customs House both come up at 2, so I'll want my grain for that. Whispered to Clark: \"Noah was one Vase and one Brush from your chair, and I paid twenty-one to make it two pieces again. I'll remember the Wall link over Province 3 on the terms you named.\"")), at="L14")
say("Rich", "Twenty-one, and worth every grain. A matched pair of Vases beside the Jar, Your Majesty, so Province 3 has the finest mantelpiece in the empire. Noah, you stopped at twenty, just as you said you would. I do admire a man who keeps to his figures.")
salaries({2:5, 4:7}, text="Salaries: Clark pays Noah 5 and Reina 7, as he declared.")

# ===== CHAPTER 4: Nothing but the Throne
# ============================== YEAR 7 ==============================
R(7, "Bad Harvest", "dribble3")
act("These are the last two rounds. As the table agreed at the start, play stops after Year 8's salaries, and everyone scores from the seat they hold then against the seat they drew (Section 18): a lower seat number is a win, the same seat a hold, a higher one a loss.")
act("Reina, the Scholar, turns the next I Ching card: Bad Harvest. A bad harvest in every province. The population holds at x2, 200 million.", map_hl=[4])
say("Reina", "\"Bad Harvest. The seed rotted, the rains came late, and the ox went lame. All provinces collect a bad harvest.\" Population holds at times two, two hundred million on the counter, so read the times-two row, bad-harvest column, all of you. Rich, the Pile's going to feel that one. And Clark, the Scholar's salary still comes due at the end of the year, rotten seed or not.",
 "Reina: A bad harvest in the second-to-last year hurts the people with the most land the most, and that isn't me. When everyone's granary shrinks together, my seventy-one looks less like a poor relation's purse and more like a vote, so I'm keeping the Brush until someone pays properly for it.")
harvest("Rich, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile: a bad year at x2. Reina's four squares of sea bring her 50, and the crown takes 30 with its canal and its own four squares. Rich, with the Reservoir, pays 10 into the pile, and Noah, with no land and no works, pays 30.")
say("Clark", "Bad harvest, so the crown is being merciful. Forty percent, and only Reina has an income to take it from. Rich, I said I'd give you a Wall link over Province 3 on the same terms, and here it is: thirty from the crown and ten from you. Noah, it's five again. It's in the book next to your name, and you've had plenty of room to read it.",
 "Clark: Rich's link costs me thirty, and in return Rich keeps buying Vases he can't use to keep Noah off my throne. Leverage is just a promise kept at the exact moment it's worth the most.")
act("The crown's year: tax at 40%, which falls only on Reina, the one province with a positive income. One project: a link of the Great Wall above Province 3, the crown offering 30 of the 40 and asking Rich for 10. Salaries at the end of the year, 5 to Noah and 7 to Reina. On new land, the crown pays 10 of every 20 this year.", map_hl=[1,3])
taxes(.40, collector="Noah, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say("Noah", "Forty percent of a bad year. Rich and I are both in the red, so we owe nothing, and Reina's fifty is the only income at the table. That's twenty from Reina, twenty in all, and it goes straight to the crown: Reina goes to 101, Clark to 156 before the Wall and the salaries. So nobody's going to say the Official skimmed on a rotten harvest, here's the count. I'm at 68.5, I still need the Brush and the Vase, and after this one there's exactly one year left. Clark, five is in your book. It's in mine too, and so is every year it's been five.",
 "Noah: There's nothing to cheat on this year. One payer, twenty grain, and I counted it twice. What I'm actually watching is Rich's two Vases and Reina's unsold Brush, because one year from the end, whoever closes a set is the one coming for my chair.")
say("Rich", "Your Majesty, a Wall over Province 3 from the crown's own hand. I'm touched, truly. But the Pile has just cost me ten grain to pay out a rotten harvest, so let me meet you in the generous middle: thirty-five from the throne, five from your humble Gentry, and I'll have it carved over the gate that the Emperor built it.",
 "Rich: One link out of four opens nothing before the table stops, so every grain I put into that Wall is grain I can't spend on Reina's Brush or count as surplus at the end. Five keeps Clark smiling, and Clark smiling is worth about five.")
say("Clark", "Thirty-five and five, done. Carve it over the gate nice and big, Rich, so Noah can read it from his province. I'm noting the five you saved in my book, under 'favours owed', and I always collect on those.",
 "Clark: Five grain is cheap rent for the one man at this table who keeps buying Vases to keep Noah away from my chair. Noah still needs the Brush and a Vase, and I've just paid the man holding the Vases.")
build("Great Wall", 40, {1:35, 3:5}, wall={3:1}, text="Public works: Clark and Rich build the first link of the Great Wall above Province 3, the crown paying 35 and Rich 5. It's the only work this year.", hover="Both pay into the Harvest Pile. One link of four above Province 3; all four would open its strip for new land. 550 of the works are still owed.")
say("Reina", "Thank you for the mercy, Your Majesty. Twenty out of fifty still feels like a rotten harvest to me, so I'll take you up on the land. Four squares of shore, half each, and Province 4 eats whatever the sky does. The Brush goes up at twenty-five. Noah and Rich, you've both told me how badly you want it, so this is where you show me.",
 "Reina: Twenty-five is a good price for Rich and a painful one for Noah at sixty-eight and a half, and if Noah buys the Brush anyway, Clark hears from me before the Vase changes hands. The land is simple arithmetic: ten a square from me, twenty back from next year's harvest, and grain in hand is how this table breaks ties.")
buy_land(4, ["I26", "I27", "J27", "K26"], {4:40, 1:40}, "New land: Reina buys four more squares of sea for Province 4, at I26, I27, J27 and K26, the crown paying half on the terms Clark declared. Each adds 20 to her harvest from next year.", hover="The 80 goes into the Harvest Pile.")
act("The auctions: three lots. Noah's Customs House and Rich's Wooden Bowl went unsold last year, so both must open at 2, with no minimum for their sellers to set. Reina puts up the Bamboo Brush, her only piece, at 25. Noah, Rich and Reina each give a limit on every lot at once; the Emperor doesn't bid.", map_hl=[2,3,4])
say("Noah", "For four years I've said my limit out loud and lost by one grain, four times. So this year I'll give you every number except that one. One more year after this, Rich has 101.5, Reina 61, and I'm at 68.5. If anyone closes a set this year, they have a year left to come for a chair, and I know whose chair is easiest to take. Reina, I'll be bidding on your Brush.",
 "Noah: I can't finish my own set now, because both Vases are sitting on Rich's squares, so the Brush is a blocking bid. I'll spend most of what I have to keep it out of Rich's hands, and just a little to slow Reina on the Bowl, because I can't afford to stop both.")
say("Rich", "A Customs House at two and a Brush at twenty-five. My dear Noah, my dear Reina, you are both far too generous to an old Gentry. I'll bid like a gentleman, and I'll take whatever the table leaves me with gratitude. And my Bowl goes to whoever loves it most.",
 "Rich: Fifty and thirty-one come to eighty-one, which leaves me the twenty I need to claim with the set next year, so both pieces at those prices puts me on the throne. If Reina wants the House past fifty she can have it, and good luck to her finding the rest.")
say("Reina", "I'm out of the shopping this year, gentlemen. One year left and no set in reach, so I'd rather keep my grain than give three quarters of it to the Pile. The Brush stays at twenty-five, and whoever pays it gets my blessing and my close attention.",
 "Reina: Buying pieces I can't finish would just be a donation to Rich's Pile, so I'm sitting on sixty-one and letting the two set-builders work out who pays for the Brush. If Rich takes the Customs House cheap at two, Clark needs to hear that, and he'll hear it from me first.")
lot(2, "CH", [B(3,2)],
 hover=TH(("", "It opened at 2, unsold last year. Rich would have gone to 50; nobody else bid."),
          ("Rich", "So Noah finally wins by one grain, and he's holding the only Brush I needed, though without a Vase he can't finish a set himself. I'll put the House at L15 with the rest of my collection and spend next year being very, very kind to the man who has my last piece.")), at="L15")
say("Rich", "A Customs House for two grain! Noah, I'll think of you every time I look at it. And congratulations on the Brush, dear fellow. One grain, of all things. The table does love its little jokes.")
act("Nobody bids on Rich's Wooden Bowl at 2. It stays in his stock for a second year, and must go up again next year at 2.", map_hl=[3])
DN7 = "Yours with my blessing. I never fight a friend over furniture."
lot(4, "BB", [B(3,31), B(3,None,DN7), B(2,32)],
 hover=TH(("", "Reina's minimum was 25. Rich stopped at 31; Noah would have gone to 60 to keep it from him."),
          ("Noah", "This wasn't a purchase, it was a blockade: the Brush is out of Rich's reach for good. But he picked up my Customs House for two, so next year I'll be watching what that does to his count.")), at="J13")
say("Noah", "Thirty-two, and for once I'm the one who won by a grain. Rich, you stopped at thirty-one, so I'll thank you for your figures the way you've thanked me for mine. That leaves me 36.5. Reina gets 8 and the Pile 24. The Brush goes on J13 beside the rest. I'm one Vase short, and both Vases are on Rich's squares, so nobody needs to worry that I'm coming for the throne.")
act("Noah has 37, not 36.5: a quarter of the Customs House's price, 0.5, came back to him as its seller.", map_hl=[2])
salaries({2:5, 4:7}, text="Salaries: Clark pays Noah 5 and Reina 7, as he declared.")

# ============================== YEAR 8 ==============================
R(8, "Population Grows by Half", "half2")
act("Reina, the Scholar, turns the next I Ching card: Population Grows by Half. A good harvest in every province, and the population grows by half to x3, 300 million: every province now eats three times what it ate in Year 1.", map_hl=[4])
say("Reina", "\"Population Grows by Half. The villages are noisier than they used to be. All provinces collect a good harvest.\" The counter goes from times two to times three, three hundred million. Read the times-three row, good-harvest column, and remember that every mouth eats half again what it ate last year. Last year of the game, gentlemen, so let's make it a fat one.",
 "Reina: A good harvest on eight squares of sea is the best card I could have turned on the last year, because new land doesn't care how many mouths there are and grain in hand is how this table breaks ties. Noah and Rich now share a board where each is one piece short with nobody left to sell to, so I'll keep reading the cards and counting.")
harvest("Rich, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile: a good year at x3. Reina, with eight squares of sea, takes 140; the crown 20; Rich, with the Reservoir, breaks even; Noah, with no land and no works, pays 20 into the pile.")
say("Clark", "The last fat year, and the crown stays at forty. Reina, that's fifty-six out of a hundred and forty, and you still keep more than anyone else at this table. Rich, a second link at the same price, because the Emperor remembers his friends. Noah, congratulations on the Brush, and on being one Vase short. It's five again. Have a look at the book.",
 "Clark: Noah needs a Vase and both of them belong to the man I'm building a Wall for, so the chair is about as safe as a chair gets. All that's left is to keep the tax low enough that nobody gets ideas about revolt, and to close the ledger with the crown on top.")
act("The crown's year: tax at 40%, which falls only on Reina. One project: the second link of the Great Wall above Province 3, the crown offering 35 of the 40 and asking Rich for 5. Salaries at the end of the year, 5 to Noah and 7 to Reina. On new land, the crown pays 10 of every 20.", map_hl=[1,3])
taxes(.40, collector="Noah, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say("Noah", "Thank you, Storyteller, it was 37 and not 36.5. I forgot my own quarter, and I've written it down. Now the count: Rich and I owe nothing, and Reina's 140 at forty percent is 56. That goes to the crown, she keeps 84, and that's all of it. I'm at 22 after this harvest. This is the last year, and the only thing still to happen is this year's auctions, so anyone who wants my chair has to finish a set in them.",
 "Noah: I drew Province 4 and I'm sitting in Province 2, so if nobody takes this chair before the salaries, that's a win. I'm watching Rich and every lot he bids on, and I only have 22 to stop him with.")
act("A set doesn't have to come from the auctions: pieces already on the board may be traded between players at any time.")
say("Rich", "My own terms, offered back to me by the Emperor himself. I could hardly refuse them, Your Majesty. Thirty-five and five, and I'll carve your name over the second gate as well. Noah, you'll want a taller ladder to read them both.",
 "Rich: The Brush is sitting on Noah's square, so my set dies one piece short, and the throne's out of reach. I'll hold Province 3, and five grain is a cheap way to keep my word to the letter while Clark writes my name in his good column.")
build("Great Wall", 40, {1:35, 3:5}, wall={3:1}, text="Public works: Clark and Rich build the second link of the Great Wall above Province 3, the crown paying 35 and Rich 5. It's the only work this year.", hover="Both pay into the Harvest Pile. Two links of four above Province 3. 510 of the works are still owed.")
say("Reina", "Land that never pays back would only feed the Pile, so I'll keep my hundred and sixty in the granary, thank you. And since the Storyteller has reminded us that pieces on the board can change hands at any time, Your Majesty, you might keep an eye on Rich's two Vases and whoever is one Vase short.",
 "Reina: The last year is no time to plant, it's time to count, and a hundred and sixty is the fattest granary I've had all night. I promised Clark an early warning if anyone built a set against him, and the only set left on this board would be a Vase passed quietly from Rich to Noah, so I've given him that warning in front of everyone.")
act("The auction: one lot. Rich's Wooden Bowl drew no bid last year, so it opens at 2 again. Noah, Rich and Reina each give their limit; the Emperor doesn't bid.", map_hl=[3])
say("Noah", "Last lot of the game, so the last count. Reina has 160, Clark 110, Rich 94.5, and I have 22. The Bowl is no use to me, since I already hold one, but it's the piece Reina still needs, and she has seven times my grain. Reina, last year you said no set was in reach. If that's still true, you won't mind me bidding.",
 "Noah: I'll put every grain I have against the one player who could use this Bowl to come for my chair. If she really wants it, 22 won't stop her, but she'll know I made her pay.")
say("Rich", "My poor little Bowl, up for a third time at two grain. Take it, somebody, for sentiment's sake. It's the last year, gentlemen, and I'd hate to go home with anything on my hands but gratitude.",
 "Rich: The Storyteller has just reminded us that pieces on the board can change hands, and Noah is sitting on twenty-two grain and a Brush that's no earthly use to him. Sixty grain buys it, leaves me the twenty I need to claim, and puts Rich Baron on the throne before Clark has finished his sums. Whispered to Noah: \"Sixty grain for the Bamboo Brush off J13, paid now and in full, to you, not to the Pile. You go from twenty-two to eighty-two in the last year, which is the best surplus you'll see all game. And my word, to the letter: with the set I will replace the Emperor and nobody else, so you keep the Official's chair, and you keep it richer. If someone outbids me, come back to me first and I'll go to seventy. Say yes and the Storyteller can record it.\"")
say("Reina", "Rich, your Bowl is a lovely thing, and it will look just as lovely beside the board as it does now. I won't spend a grain on a set I can't finish before the lamps go out.",
 "Reina: A Bowl is no use without three more pieces I'll never get, and every grain I don't spend counts if it comes down to a tie. My vote at this table is the hundred and sixty in my granary, and it isn't going to the Pile.")
act("Nobody bids on Rich's Wooden Bowl at 2, for the second year running. It stays in his stock as the table's last round ends its auctions.", map_hl=[3])
say("Noah", "I'll say it out loud, because that's how I've played all game. Rich has offered me sixty for the Brush on J13, and I'm taking it. That puts me at 82 and Rich at 34.5 with a full set. He's given me his word he'll take the throne and not my chair, and words aren't binding, so here's the arithmetic either way: I drew Province 4, so Province 2 or Province 3 is still a win for me. Clark, I'm sorry. Reina warned you, and so am I.",
 "Noah: Whatever Rich does with it, I'm sitting lower than the 4 I drew, so the Brush can't cost me the win, and sixty grain in the last year is the biggest surplus I'll ever see. It isn't kind to Clark, but five a year for eight years was never kind to me either. Whispered to Rich: \"Done, sixty, and I'm saying it to the table. I'm holding you to the throne, Rich, but I've already worked out what happens if you don't.\"")
act("A trade: Rich pays Noah 60 for the Bamboo Brush, which moves from Noah's J13 to Rich's K15, beside his Customs House.", deltas={pid(3):-60, pid(2):60}, map_hl=[2,3], deal=True)
give(pid(2), pid(3), "BB", "K15")
act("Rich now holds a complete set for the Gentry: the Dragon Jar, a Vase, the Customs House and the Bamboo Brush, with 34.5 grain. He may declare it and change seats.", map_hl=[3])
say("Rich", "Your Majesty, I have adored you from the first harvest to the last, and I adore you still. But a Jar, a Vase, a House and a Brush make a set, and a set makes an Emperor. I'll take the throne, and nothing but the throne, exactly as I told Noah. Clark, the Gentry's seat is a warm one. The Pile is yours to pay out, with my compliments.")
swap(3, 1, "Richard Baron declares a complete set (Dragon Jar, Vase, Customs House, Bamboo Brush) and 34.5 grain, and replaces Clark Grafton as Emperor. Rich becomes Emperor of Province 1; Clark becomes Gentry of Province 3. All Rich's pieces go back to stock: the Dragon Jar and the Customs House to Province 2's, both Vases and the Brush to Province 4's. Rich takes the crown's 110; Clark draws 60 from the Harvest Pile.",
 hover="Rich: I said I'd take the throne and no one else's chair, and that's exactly what I've done. Province 3 to the crown is a win, and Clark's hundred and ten on top of my thirty-four makes it a comfortable one.",
 at={})
say("Rich", "My first act as Emperor, and my last: five to the Official and five to the Scholar, as the law sets it down. Noah, you'll find it's the same figure you've had in the book all game, so nothing surprises you. Reina, Clark's seven was Clark's promise. I'm only bound by my own, and I've kept every one of those to the letter.",
 "Rich: The minimum keeps a hundred and thirty-four and a half in the crown's reserve, and grain breaks ties. I'm buying no land, because land bought tonight never pays a single harvest.")
salaries({2:5, 4:5}, text="Salaries: Rich, now the Emperor, pays Noah 5 and Reina 5.")
act("The table stops here, after Year 8's salaries, as it agreed at the start, and scores from where everyone sits against the seat they drew (Section 18). Rich Baron, who drew the Gentry (3), sits on the throne (1), with 134.5 in the crown's reserve. Noah Tall, who drew the Scholar (4), is the Official (2), with 87. Clark Grafton, who drew the Official (2), is the Gentry (3), with 60. Reina Osorio, who drew the throne (1), is the Scholar (4), with 165.", big=True)

RESULT = export(
 "The table stopped after eight rounds, as it agreed before setup (Note 19), with 300 of the 810 owed in public works built, so this is neither survival nor collapse. Players are ranked by the seat they finished in against the one they drew, with grain breaking ties.",
 ["Rich drew the Gentry and finished on the throne. He sold his Land Deed at 95 in Year 5, bought Reina's Vase at 21 in Year 6 to keep it from Noah, and took the Customs House at 2 when it reopened in Year 7. In the last round he paid Noah 60 for the Brush, a private trade of a piece on the board, and replaced Clark, taking the throne and nothing else, as he had promised.",
  "Noah drew the Scholar and finished as the Official. He completed a set in Year 5 with the Deed at 95, and chose the Official's chair over the throne, since on the throne his Deed would have gone back to Rich's stock, where Reina, one Deed short, could buy it. In Year 7 he paid 32 for the Brush to keep it from Rich. Then, with 22 grain in the last round, he sold it to Rich for 60, knowing that any seat but the Scholar's was still a win for him.",
  "Clark drew the Official, won the Deed and the Brush in Year 3 and took the throne from Reina, then held it for five years at 50%, 60% and then 40% tax. He built the Winter Palace, the Province 3 Reservoir and the Marble Pagoda, and the first two Wall links above Province 3. He lost the throne to the one trade the auction couldn't block, and finished in the Gentry's seat.",
  "Reina drew the throne and lost it in Year 3, then fell from the Official's chair to the Scholar's when Noah completed his set in Year 5. As the Scholar she bought eight squares of sea, the crown paying most of the cost, and finished with the largest purse at the table, 165, holding nothing. Her warning to Clark about the Vase came in the last round, in front of everyone.",
  "Every seller held back its own piece, the one both rivals needed, for as long as the rules allowed. The unsold-lot rule came into play only in Year 6, when the Customs House and the Bowl drew no bid at 150. The Customs House then sold at 2, and the Bowl drew no bid at 2 in Years 7 and 8 and ended the game unsold."])
